), terminal UI (tool renderers for every state, above-editor widgets, statuslines and footers, editor-replacing settings panels with staged user/project/runtime scopes, model and thinking-level pickers, mouse interaction, theming, elevation, motion and render performance), skills, prompt templates, themes, packages, settings, RPC and the SDK. Use whenever the task names pi, a pi extension or package, `ctx.ui`, `pi.registerTool`/`registerCommand`, `renderCall`/`renderResult`, or asks why a pi TUI looks or feels wrong. Not for Raspberry Pi hardware or other agent frameworks.","source":"skill","sourceInfo":{"path":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent/skills/extending-pi-agent/SKILL.md","source":"auto","scope":"user","origin":"top-level","baseDir":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent"}},{"name":"skill:selecting-worker-model","description":"Selects a worker model, exact Pi model ID, and thinking level for coding and research tasks. Use when delegating work or changing a model override.","source":"skill","sourceInfo":{"path":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent/skills/selecting-worker-model/SKILL.md","source":"auto","scope":"user","origin":"top-level","baseDir":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent"}},{"name":"skill:reddit","description":"Use the reddit-rs MCP server to read, search and research Reddit: browse subreddit feeds, search posts and communities, fetch a post with its comment tree, summarise or compare threads, look up users and community metadata, manage saved items, and run background monitors. Reach for this whenever a task involves Reddit, a subreddit, r/ or u/ names, a reddit.com link or thread, community opinion or sentiment on a topic, or 'what do people say about X' — even when Reddit is never named explicitly. Covers the tool inventory, token-efficient call patterns, which credential mode each tool needs, elicitation-gated writes, the resource URI templates, the four prompts, and every failure mode with its fix.","source":"skill","sourceInfo":{"path":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent/skills/reddit/SKILL.md","source":"auto","scope":"user","origin":"top-level","baseDir":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent"}},{"name":"skill:reddit-cli","description":"Drive Reddit through the `reddit` command-line binary when the reddit MCP tools are unavailable, and reach the capabilities the MCP surface never exposes: layered TOML configuration, shell completions, the background token-refresh service, response-cache maintenance, saved-history sync, JSON/JSONL/Markdown archive export, local full-text search, multireddits, private messages, multi-account switching and manual session capture. Use this whenever Reddit work must go through a shell — no MCP server configured, a script or CI job, an `--offline` cached read, or a task that names the `reddit` command — and for translating any reddit MCP tool call into its exact CLI equivalent. Covers the tool-to-command map, the `--output json` scripting contract, every exit code, worked `jq` one-liners, and the credential and write-safety rules.","source":"skill","sourceInfo":{"path":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent/skills/reddit-cli/SKILL.md","source":"auto","scope":"user","origin":"top-level","baseDir":"/home/furbyhaxx/.pi/agent"}},{"name":"skill:reverse-engineer-anything","description":"Reverse engineer native, managed, Electron/JavaScript, packaged, firmware, and browser targets with REA. Use shipped-artifact or requested runtime evidence to explain features, compare versions, decompile code, or guide a reconstruction. Skip REA for ordinary source-repository architecture analysis.","source":"skill","sourceInfo":{"path":"/home/furbyhaxx/.agents/skills/reverse-engineer-anything/SKILL.md","source":"auto","scope":"user","origin":"top-level","baseDir":"/home/furbyhaxx/.agents"}},{"name":"skill:online-research","description":"Use whenever the answer could depend on information outside the current context or on facts that may have changed since model training. Trigger by default for technical questions, documentation lookups, APIs, libraries, tools, produ